7 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049828 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050577 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050186 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050577 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050223 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050577 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186110725 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050577 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186110726 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050577 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186110727 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050577 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186110728 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050577 #edb/ref 1031 "The Average of the Roots Formula" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050577 #edb/ref 1033 1451 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050577 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "5555aaf2-f73f-5c86-8f74-5c76cb5a6f50" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186082942 #edb/ref 1069 "e-14835" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186082942 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the axis of symmetry for the parabola $y=2x^2-12x+3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186082942 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "17f80459-f98c-5f34-acd4-6407c047615e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186082942 #edb/ref 1074 "The equation of the axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=ax^2+bx+c$ is given by\n\n$$\nx = -\\dfrac{b}{2a}.\n$$\n\nWe substitute $a=2$ and $b=-12$ into the formula for the axis of symmetry. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{(-12)}{2(2)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{(-12)}{4} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the axis of symmetry is $x=3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186082943 #edb/ref 1069 "e-14836" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186082943 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola $y=-x^2+4x+1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186082943 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f4cf6319-af75-53c4-a5a8-87478b7c3fd0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186082943 #edb/ref 1074 "The vertex of the parabola lies on the axis of symmetry, so we will start by computing the axis of symmetry. \n\nThe equation of the axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=ax^2+bx+c$ is given by\n\n$$\nx = -\\dfrac{b}{2a}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=-1$ and $b=4$ into the formula for the axis of symmetry, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{4}{2(-1)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{(-2)} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the axis of symmetry is $x=2.$ This also represents the $x$-coordinate of the vertex. To find the $y$-coordinate of the vertex, we can substitute $x=2$ into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-{x}^{2}+4x+1 \\\\  & =-(2)^{2}+4(2)+1 \\\\  & =-4+8+1 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertex is at $(2,5).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186082944 #edb/ref 1069 "e-14837" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186082944 #edb/ref 1071 "The axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=0.25x^2+kx+2$ is $x=-6.$ Find the value of $k.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186082944 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dad0da4b-af03-507c-9549-6f5963783da6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186082944 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we will calculate the axis of symmetry in terms of $k.$ Then, we will set our result equal to $-6.$\n\nThe equation of the axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=ax^2+bx+c$ is given by\n\n$$\nx = -\\dfrac{b}{2a}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=0.25$ and $b=k$ into the formula for the axis of symmetry, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{k}{2(0.25)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{k}{0.5} \\\\  & =-2k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe already know that the axis of symmetry is $x=-6,$ so we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2k & =-6 \\\\ k & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083186 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049919 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083186 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186083620 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083186 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186083655 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083186 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186540026 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083186 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186540082 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083186 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Axis of Symmetry of a Parabola" #edb/r