7592186539939 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540026 #edb/ref 1069 "q-222074" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540026 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540026 #edb/ref 1071 "The axis of symmetry of the parabola $y={x}^{2}-8x-7$ is $x=${{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540026 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "164d94c5-b0eb-592e-af54-44af80279820" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540026 #edb/ref 1074 "The equation of the axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=a{x}^{2}+bx+c$ is given by\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{b}{2a}.\n$$\n\nWe substitute $a=1$ and $b=-8$ into the formula for the axis of symmetry. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{(-8)}{2(1)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{(-8)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{2} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the axis of symmetry is $x=4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540055 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539993 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540055 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106547" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540055 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540055 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola $y=-2{x}^{2}+4x-9.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540055 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "64f1282c-c0b1-511f-b876-dbf03d7ec8a1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540055 #edb/ref 1074 "The vertex of the parabola lies on the axis of symmetry, so we will start by computing the axis of symmetry. \n\nThe equation of the axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=a{x}^{2}+bx+c$ is given by\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{b}{2a}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=-2$ and $b=4$ into the formula for the axis of symmetry, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{4}{2(-2)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{(-4)} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the axis of symmetry is $x=1.$ This also represents the $x$-coordinate of the vertex. To find the $y$-coordinate of the vertex, we substitute $x=1$ into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-2{x}^{2}+4x-9 \\\\  & =-2(1)^{2}+4(1)-9 \\\\  & =-2+4-9 \\\\  & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertex is at $(1,-7).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540082 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539963 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540082 #edb/ref 1069 "q-222079" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540082 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540082 #edb/ref 1071 "The axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=-2{x}^{2}+10x-3$ is $x=${{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540082 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b00cf238-5726-569e-8376-bed95c1380a1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540082 #edb/ref 1074 "The equation of the axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=a{x}^{2}+bx+c$ is given by\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{b}{2a}.\n$$\n\nWe substitute $a=-2$ and $b=10$ into the formula for the axis of symmetry. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{10}{2(-2)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{10}{(-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the axis of symmetry is $x=\\frac{5}{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540085 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539948 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540085 #edb/ref 1069 "q-13389" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540085 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540085 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola $y=3{x}^{2}-18x-3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540085 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b9ff66d7-f50d-5b18-a934-a31f261f86c6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540085 #edb/ref 1074 "The vertex of the parabola lies on the axis of symmetry, so we will start by computing the axis of symmetry. \n\nThe equation of the axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=a{x}^{2}+bx+c$ is given by\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{b}{2a}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=3$ and $b=-18$ into the formula for the axis of symmetry, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{(-18)}{2(3)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{(-18)}{6} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the axis of symmetry is $x=3.$ This also represents the $x$-coordinate of the vertex. To find the $y$-coordinate of the vertex, we substitute $x=3$ into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3{x}^{2}-18x-3 \\\\  & =3(3)^{2}-18(3)-3 \\\\  & =27-54-3 \\\\  & =-30\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertex is at $(3,-30).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540089 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539971 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540089 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106548" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540089 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540089 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola $y=-4{x}^{2}-24x-10.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540089 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c18c5b5e-e1be-5b37-80b1-242d38a7007c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540089 #edb/ref 1074 "The vertex of the parabola lies on the axis of symmetry, so we will start by computing the axis of symmetry. \n\nThe equation of the axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=a{x}^{2}+bx+c$ is given by\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{b}{2a}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=-4$ and $b=-24$ into the formula for the axis of symmetry, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{(-24)}{2(-4)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{(-24)}{(-8)} \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the axis of symmetry is $x=-3.$ This also represents the $x$-coordinate of the vertex. To find the $y$-coordinate of the vertex, we substitute $x=-3$ into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-4{x}^{2}-24x-10 \\\\  & =-4(-3)^{2}-24(-3)-10 \\\\  & =-36+72-10 \\\\  & =26\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertex is at $(-3,26).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540093 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539990 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540093 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186540020 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540093 #edb/ref 1069 "q-222087" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540093 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540093 #edb/ref 1071 "What are the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola $y=-{x}^{2}-4x-3?$\n\nThe coordinates of the vertex are\n$($\n{{field-1}}\n,\n{{field-2}}\n$).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540093 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c719977b-e48f-55fa-971c-b6c55e94e98c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540093 #edb/ref 1074 "The parabola's vertex lies on the symmetry axis, so we will start by computing the axis of symmetry. \n\nThe equation of the axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=a{x}^{2}+bx+c$ is given by\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{b}{2a}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=-1$ and $b=-4$ into the formula for the axis of symmetry, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{(-4)}{2(-1)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{(-4)}{(-2)} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the axis of symmetry is $x=-2.$ This also represents the $x$-coordinate of the vertex. To find the $y$-coordinate of the vertex, we substitute $x=-2$ into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-{x}^{2}-4x-3 \\\\  & =-(-2)^{2}-4(-2)-3 \\\\  & =-4+8-3 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertex is at $(-2,1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540095 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186540013 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540095 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106551" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540095 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540095 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/83/830944c0cff8aa0b2f1c58c9cd2ab7ae0f2560a9aa0e244796c0814ec99fd0c3.png)\n\nFind the value of $k$ for the parabola $y=k{x}^{2}-4x-3,$ shown above." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540095 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c9db5029-028a-58a7-87ff-11b09d5541ba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540095 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we will calculate the axis of symmetry in terms of $k.$ Then, since the graph tells us that the axis of symmetry is $x=2,$ we will set our result equal to $2.$ \n\nThe equation of the axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=a{x}^{2}+bx+c$ is given by\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{b}{2a}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=k$ and $b=-4$ into the formula for the axis of symmetry, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{(-4)}{2(k)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2k} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe already know that the axis of symmetry is $x=2,$ so we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{k} & =2 \\\\ k & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540108 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539978 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540108 #edb/ref 1069 "q-222095" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540108 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540108 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/8e/8ea5d11ca8b92193517fa7082a466e2ece1f5c505e6881c70ce8ebebb5b93913.png)\n\nFind the value of $k$ for the parabola $y=5{x}^{2}+kx-8,$ shown above.\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540108 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f5ac6aea-bcfe-5a0e-83c5-58a351f6e0ff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540108 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we will calculate the axis of symmetry in terms of $k.$ Then, since the graph tells us that the axis of symmetry is $x=-2,$ we will set our result equal to $-2.$ \n\nThe equation of the axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=a{x}^{2}+bx+c$ is given by\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{b}{2a}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=5$ and $b=k$ into the formula for the axis of symmetry, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{k}{2(5)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{k}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe already know that the axis of symmetry is $x=-2,$ so we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{k}{10} & =-2 \\\\ -k & =-20 \\\\ k & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
